---
import Button from "../workshop/Button.astro";
import Disclosure from "../workshop/Disclosure.astro";
import InteractiveFrame from "../workshop/InteractiveFrame.astro";
---

<h2 id="connect-model">Connect your local model</h2>
<ul class="workshop-intro-list">
  <li>
    <strong>Open your app and load your model.</strong> Use LM Studio, Llama App,
    or Ollama. Load the chat model you want to work with so it is ready to receive
    requests from these examples.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Start the local API server.</strong> In
    <a href="https://lmstudio.ai/docs/developer/core/server">LM Studio</a>, open
    the Developer tab and press “Start server.” You can also run
    <code>lms server start --port 1234</code> in a terminal.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Select your app below, then find your model.</strong> The app menu fills
    in its usual server address. For LM Studio, that is
    <code>http://127.0.0.1:1234</code>. Press “Find models,” then choose your
    model from the menu. This connection is shared by all the examples on the
    page.
  </li>
</ul>
<InteractiveFrame label="Local model connection" sticker="connect here!">
  <div class="workshop-connection">
    <label class="workshop-field"
      >App<select id="preset"
        ><option value="llama">Llama App</option><option value="ollama"
          >Ollama</option
        ><option value="lmstudio">LM Studio</option></select
      ></label
    >
    <label class="workshop-field"
      >Server<input id="base" type="url" value="http://127.0.0.1:9931" /></label
    >
    <label class="workshop-field"
      >Model<select id="model"
        ><option value="">Find models first</option></select
      ></label
    >
  </div>
  <div class="workshop-actions">
    <Button id="connect">Find models</Button><p
      id="connection-status"
      role="status"
    >
      One connection for every example below.
    </p>
  </div>
  <Disclosure title="Connection help and embedding setup"
    ><p>
      The local API server must be running. Use the same address as <a
        href="/week-12/llama/">Week 12</a
      >. On the published class site, Llama App may block a direct browser
      connection. If that happens, <a
        href="/llm-playground/downloads/llm-playground-all.zip"
        download>download the workshops</a
      >, unzip the folder, and run <code>npm start</code>. Keep that terminal
      running and press “Find models” here again. The local workshop server acts
      as a bridge for this class site, forwarding only model-list, chat, and
      embedding requests to your local model. You can also open <code
        >http://127.0.0.1:3213</code
      > to work entirely from the downloaded copy. If the browser asks for local
      network access, this workshop needs that permission to connect.
    </p><p>
      The optional semantic retrieval mode in RAG needs a separate embedding
      model. Download <a
        href="https://huggingface.co/unsloth/embeddinggemma-300m-GGUF/tree/main"
        >EmbeddingGemma as GGUF for Llama</a
      >, or find it in <a href="https://ollama.com/library/embeddinggemma"
        >Ollama’s model library</a
      >. With Ollama, run <code>ollama pull embeddinggemma</code>, use port
      11434 and model name <code>embeddinggemma</code>. With Llama, run <code
        >llama serve -hf unsloth/embeddinggemma-300m-GGUF --embedding --port
        9932</code
      >. In LM Studio, load an embedding model alongside the chat model, use
      <code>http://127.0.0.1:1234</code> for the embedding server, and enter its
      model identifier.
    </p><div class="workshop-connection">
      <label class="workshop-field"
        >Embedding server<input
          id="embedding-base"
          value="http://127.0.0.1:9932"
        /></label
      ><label class="workshop-field"
        >Embedding model (blank = first model)<input
          id="embedding-model"
        /></label
      >
    </div>
  </Disclosure>
</InteractiveFrame>
